 r←ReadmesAreDue registryPath;ns;filename;lastSweep;indexStamp;qdmx
⍝ Tells whether it is worth walking the Registry looking for READMEs to fetch.
⍝ Two things make it worth doing, and nothing else does:
⍝ * something has been published since the last walk, so there is a new package to look at
⍝ * 24 hours have passed, which is when `ReadmeIsDue` lets failed packages round again
⍝ Anything else and this returns 0 having done a single stat, whatever the size of the
⍝ Registry. The walk itself is expensive - a config and a record read per package - so it
⍝ must not happen on every housekeeping call.
⍝ The record holds `indexStamp` (the index file's timestamp as at the last completed walk)
⍝ and `lastSweep` (when that walk finished), both in seconds. No record means never walked.
 r←1
 filename←GetReadmeSweepFilename registryPath
 :If F.IsFile filename
     :Trap 11 19 22
         ns←⎕JSON⍠('Dialect' 'JSON5')⊣⊃F.NGET filename
         lastSweep←{0=ns.⎕NC ⍵:0 ⋄ ns⍎⍵}'lastSweep'
         indexStamp←{0=ns.⎕NC ⍵:0 ⋄ ns⍎⍵}'indexStamp'
     :Else
         qdmx←⎕DMX
         LogError'Unreadable README sweep record "',filename,'": ',qdmx.EM,'; RC=',⍕qdmx.EN
         :Return                                    ⍝ Walk anyway; the record gets rewritten
     :EndTrap
     :If indexStamp=GetIndexStamp registryPath
     :AndIf (⊃20 ⎕DT⊂⎕TS)<lastSweep+24×3600
         r←0
     :EndIf
 :EndIf
